Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: for official uses (taxes, Social Security, employment verification, benefits), yes — the name on the document should match the name the Social Security Administration (SSA) has for your SSN. Mismatches can cause delays, rejected forms, or notices.

Details and practical steps

- Why exact match matters
  - SSA and IRS use the SSN + name pair to verify identity. If the name you give doesn’t match the name on file for that SSN, the agency or employer may flag a “name/SSN mismatch.”
  - Common places this matters: Social Security applications and benefits, W‑2s and tax returns, Form I‑9/employment verification, mortgage/loan underwriting, and many government forms.

- Minor variations that sometimes pass
  - Middle initial vs full middle name, omission or inclusion of a middle name, use of a hyphen, or minor spacing differences sometimes are tolerated. But suffixes (Jr./Sr./III), legal last‑name changes (marriage/divorce), or very different spellings often trigger a mismatch.

- If your name doesn’t match SSA records
  - Best option: update SSA records so your legal name matches the name you use. You can do this through the SSA website (create a “my Social Security” account), by visiting an SSA office, or by calling 1‑800‑772‑1213. You’ll need original or certified documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate).
  - If you can’t update the SSA record immediately, tell the requesting organization you have a name difference and supply supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, driver’s license) as they request. Some organizations allow an explanation plus proof; others will require you to correct SSA records first.

- For tax returns specifically
  - The IRS compares names to SSA records. A mismatch can trigger a letter and delay in processing refunds. Fixing the SSA record before filing prevents that.

- Privacy/security note
  - Only share the full SSN on secure, required forms and channels. When possible, provide only the last four digits unless a full SSN is explicitly required.

If you want, tell me:
- What type of form you’re filling out (employer form, tax form, loan, government benefit, etc.), and
- Which version of your name is on your SSA record versus the name on the document.

I can then suggest the best immediate action (update SSA, attach proof, or draft a short explanation you can send with the form).

