Short answer: it depends on the requester — but for anything that is matched against Social Security Administration (SSA) or IRS records (employment forms, W‑2, tax forms, benefits, background/identity verification), your name should match the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). If they ask for “name as shown on Social Security card” or “legal name,” make them match exactly to avoid delays or “no‑match” problems.

Guidance and practical steps
- Exact-match required: If the form is verified electronically against SSA/IRS data (employers, tax/benefits agencies), use the exact name on your Social Security card (including suffixes like Jr./Sr.). A mismatch can trigger a no‑match, delay processing, or require correction.
- Minor differences often OK: Small punctuation differences (periods, commas, hyphens) or adding/omitting a middle initial are usually tolerated by humans and some systems, but automated checks might still fail on some variations.
- Problematic differences: Different last name (maiden vs married), missing or different suffix (Jr./III), or a different first name (Bill vs William) often cause failures and should be resolved.
- What to do if names don’t match:
  - Best: either update the form to match your SSA record, or update your SSA record to your legal current name (see below).
  - If you must submit before you can update SSA, contact the requester (HR, agency, bank) and tell them there’s a name discrepancy; ask whether they’ll accept supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, driver’s license) and whether you should attach them.
- How to update SSA: to change the name on your Social Security record you generally need proof of identity and proof of the legal name change (e.g., marriage certificate, divorce decree, or court order). Visit the SSA website (ssa.gov) or your local Social Security office for exact documents and procedures. You can also call SSA to make an appointment.
- Security note: avoid sending your full SSN over unsecured email or forms. Provide only what’s requested and use secure portals when possible. You’ve shared only the last four here — that’s a safer practice.

If you tell me who the requester is (employer, bank, government agency) and how the form phrases the “name” field (e.g., “legal name,” “name as shown on Social Security card”), I can give more specific advice and sample wording to include with the form if you need to explain a discrepancy.

