Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: ideally yes — your name on any form that will be checked against Social Security records should match exactly the name the Social Security Administration (SSA) has for your SSN. Mismatches can cause delays, rejected forms, tax/earnings-reporting problems, or requests for documentation.

Details and practical guidance
- Why exact matching matters
  - SSA/IRS systems match SSN + name to link earnings and tax records. A mismatch can trigger notices, incorrect wage credits, or problems issuing tax forms (W-2/1099).
  - Employers, banks, and government agencies often verify your SSN against SSA records; small differences may require manual verification.

- What counts as a “mismatch”
  - Differences in spelling, use/omission of a middle name or initial, hyphens, suffixes (Jr., III), or use of a maiden vs. married name can all cause mismatches.
  - Minor formatting differences (e.g., with/without periods in initials) are sometimes tolerated, but don’t rely on that for important forms.

- Common situations
  - Tax returns / W-2 / 1099: Name must match SSA record; otherwise the IRS may send a CP notice or reject/flag the return.
  - Employment (I-9/HR onboarding): Employers need documents that establish identity and work authorization. Employers also use the name/SSN to report wages; consistency is best.
  - Banking / loan / benefits: Institutions may accept small variations, but may ask for additional ID if they can’t verify.

- If your name does not match SSA records
  - Best fix: update your name with the SSA so their record matches your current legal name (apply for a corrected SSN card). You’ll need proof of identity and proof of the name change (marriage certificate, court order, etc.).
