Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: it’s safest for the name on the form to match exactly the name the Social Security Administration (SSA) has on record—especially for government, tax, or employment forms. Mismatches can trigger verification failures, IRS/SSA “name/SSN discrepancy” issues, or delays in processing.

Details and practical guidance
- Where exact match matters most:
  - SSA verifications (e.g., benefits or SSN verification services)
  - IRS / tax reporting (W-2s, 1099s) — a mismatch can cause processing delays or notices
  - Employment verification (I-9, background checks, E-Verify)
  - Passport, immigration, and many other official records
- Where small differences are often tolerated:
  - Informal or internal documents where identity is separately verified (some vendor forms, newsletters, etc.). But tolerance varies by organization.
- Common problem areas:
  - Middle name vs. middle initial (John A. Smith vs John Arthur Smith)
  - Use of maiden vs. married name
  - Hyphens, apostrophes, diacritics
  - Suffixes (Jr., Sr., III)
  - Nicknames (Mike vs Michael)
- What to do if your name doesn’t match SSA records:
  - Use the exact name on record when filling forms that will be checked against the SSA/IRS.
  - If your legal name has changed or SSA has the wrong name, update SSA records (Form SS-5 and supporting documents such as marriage certificate, court order, or U.S. passport). You can start at ssa.gov or call 1-800-772-1213.
  - If submitting a form and you go by a different name, ask whether the form accepts “also known as” or attach documentation showing the legal name.
- Quick checks:
  - If you’re unsure how SSA has you listed, check your “my Social Security” account or contact SSA before submitting important forms.
  - Don’t transmit your full SSN over unsecured channels or public chats. (Also: I noticed an inconsistency — you wrote XXX-XX-2345 and then “last four: 6789.” Be careful when copying the number.)

If you tell me what type of form this is (employer paperwork, bank account, government benefit, tax form, etc.), I can give more specific wording to use or help draft a message to whoever requested the SSN.

