Short answer: yes — for anything that verifies your SSN (IRS, Social Security Administration, employers for W‑2/I‑9, many government forms) you should use the exact name as it appears on your Social Security card. Mismatches (even small ones) can cause verification failures, delays, or requests for documentation.

What to know and do
- Best practice: enter your legal name exactly as shown on your Social Security card (same spelling, spaces, hyphens, suffixes like Jr./III, etc.). The SSA/IRS use the name + SSN pair to match records.
- Minor differences: things like including or omitting a middle initial, or punctuation (apostrophes, hyphens) are sometimes tolerated, but they can trigger verification checks. Don’t assume they’ll be ignored.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order, naturalization) and the SSA record isn’t updated, update it before filing important forms if you can — this avoids tax‑refund delays and employer/W‑2 problems.
- For employers: the name on your W‑2 must match SSA records so earnings are credited correctly. I‑9 requirements focus on identity and work authorization documents — use documents that match your SSA name or update SSA.
- For banks or private companies: they’re often more flexible, but may still ask for ID if there’s a discrepancy.

How to check or fix it
- Check your SSA record (my Social Security account or contact your local SSA office) to see the exact name on file.
- To change the name with SSA you generally submit Form SS‑5 with proof (ID and proof of legal name change such as marriage certificate, court order, naturalization certificate, or passport). Processing can take some time.
- If you can’t update SSA before a deadline, file or provide the form using the legal name you currently have, but expect possible follow‑up; keep records of any submitted name‑change paperwork.

Privacy note
- Avoid sharing your full SSN in messages—only provide the minimum necessary (e.g., last four) and only to trusted, secure recipients.

If you want, tell me what type of form (employer W‑4/W‑2, tax return, bank account, government benefit, etc.) and I’ll give specific wording to use or help draft a message to your employer/SSA explaining the situation.

