Short answer: yes — in practice you should use the exact legal name that appears in the Social Security Administration (SSA) records (the name on your Social Security card) whenever a form ties a name to your SSN. Using a different spelling or name form can cause verification problems.

What to know and why it matters
- SSA/IRS matching: Agencies and employers verify name + SSN together. If the name on a tax or payroll form doesn’t match SSA records, it can trigger a mismatch that delays processing (tax refunds, W‑2/1099 reporting, earning credits).
- Banks and ID checks: Financial institutions and government agencies often require the name to match the name on your government ID/SSA record. Small variations sometimes pass, but not guaranteed.
- Legal documents: For contracts or legal filings, use your full legal name (the one on your ID or SSA record) so there’s no question of identity.
- Small differences: Minor variations (use of/omission of a middle initial, presence/absence of a period, small punctuation differences) are often tolerated, but things like nicknames (Bob vs. Robert), different last‑name spellings, missing suffixes (Jr./Sr.), or a changed last name usually cause problems.

