Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: it depends — but when an organization verifies your SSN against Social Security Administration (SSA) records (or the IRS), the name on their form generally needs to match your legal name as it appears in SSA’s records. Small differences (middle initial vs full middle name, punctuation, spacing) are sometimes tolerated, but differences in last name, use of nicknames, or missing suffixes (Jr./III) can cause a mismatch and delays.

Guidance and common situations
- SSA / IRS / payroll / benefits / E‑Verify: These usually require the name to match SSA records. Mismatches can trigger verification failures, tax/W‑2 problems, or delays in benefits or hiring.
- Banks and lenders: They usually want your government ID to match the name you provide; exact SSA match is less strict unless they’re verifying identity against SSA.
- Applications/forms that just collect SSN: If the form is internal only and not being matched to SSA, exact spelling may not be enforced — but it’s safest to use the legal name on your SSN card or government ID.
- Name changes: If you changed your name (marriage, divorce, court), update your SSA record before submitting forms that will be verified.

What you can do
1. Use the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card when possible. That avoids mismatches.
2. If the form uses a different spelling/format and you can’t change it, ask the organization whether they require an exact SSA match or whether the variation is acceptable.
3. If you need to change your SSA record, contact the SSA or visit ssa.gov to find required documents and how to update your name.
4. Keep sharing of your full SSN minimal — provide only the last four digits when possible. You already used only the last four (6789), which is safer.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether the name variation is acceptable (you can paste the two name versions and I’ll write it).
- Give the SSA’s name-change/document checklist or a template for updating your SSA record.

Which would you like? Also tell me what type of form (employer, bank, government benefit, tax, etc.) and the two name spellings (without sharing extra sensitive data) so I can tailor the guidance.

