Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — the name on a form should match the name on the Social Security record for anything that will be reported or verified against your SSN (payroll, tax forms, benefits). Minor differences sometimes pass, but mismatches can cause delays or "name/SSN mismatch" problems.

Details and practical guidance
- Why it matters: employers, the IRS, and other agencies verify your SSN against Social Security Administration (SSA) records. If the SSA has a different spelling or format, earnings or tax filings can be rejected or put on hold until the mismatch is resolved.
- What tends to be acceptable:
  - Middle name vs. middle initial (John A. Smith vs John Allen Smith) often OK.
  - Omission of punctuation (McDonald vs Mc Donald) usually OK.
  - Accents/diacritics are typically ignored.
- What commonly causes problems:
  - Different last name (married vs maiden) unless SSA record updated.
  - Suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) included on one record but not the other.
  - Significant spelling errors or transposed letters.
- For specific forms:
  - Payroll, W-2, 1099 and benefit enrollment: use the exact name as it appears on your Social Security card/SSA record.
  - Bank or ID forms: use whatever matches your legal ID (driver’s license, passport). If the bank needs to verify SSN, SSA match rules apply.
  - If a form only asks for the last four digits of your SSN, that’s usually sufficient for the form — but if the organization later verifies the full SSN they may still check the name against SSA records.

What to do if names don’t match
1. Check your SSA record: sign into your “my Social Security” account or contact SSA to see the name they have on file.
2. If SSA has the wrong name, update it before you submit critical documents (bring proof: marriage certificate, court order, passport, etc.).
3. If you can’t update immediately, use the exact name on your Social Security card when submitting anything that will be reported to SSA or the IRS, and notify the receiving organization that you’re in the process of correcting your SSA record.
4. Keep proof of any name-change documents handy in case an employer or agency asks.

Privacy note
- Avoid sending your full SSN over insecure channels (email, chat). If you must provide it, use the organization’s secure form or a trusted phone line.

If you want, tell me:
- Which form it is (payroll, bank, benefits, government) and the two name versions you’re comparing (don’t include your full SSN). I can give advice tailored to that situation or help draft a message to HR/SSA.

