Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — use the exact name as it appears on your Social Security card (or in SSA records). Many agencies and tax/benefit systems verify the name + SSN together, and differences can cause rejects, delays, or mismatch notices.

Details and practical guidance
- Best practice: enter the name exactly as shown on your Social Security card (first name, middle name/initial, last name, and any suffix like Jr./III). If you don’t have the card handy, you can check your name in your My Social Security account or request a replacement card from the SSA.
- Which differences matter:
  - Important: different last name, missing or different suffix (Jr./Sr./III), or a different last name (maiden vs. married) will likely cause a mismatch.
  - Often tolerated: minor formatting differences (hyphens, periods, extra spaces) or using a middle initial vs full middle name sometimes pass, but not guaranteed.
- Where mismatches cause problems: payroll and employer records, W-2/1099 filings, IRS processing (name/SSN mismatches trigger CP notices and possible backup withholding), benefits claims, and background checks.
- If your legal name changed and SSA hasn’t been updated: update SSA first (you may need to provide documentation) before submitting forms that require exact matching.
- If you can’t match exactly right now:
  - Add a note on the form if there’s an area for “as shown on Social Security card” or “other names used.”
  - Attach a copy of your SSN card or government ID if the requester allows attachments.
  - Contact the organization and ask whether the minor difference they see is acceptable or whether they need SSA-confirmed name.
- Privacy reminder: avoid sending your full SSN over insecure channels (email/chat). You already masked it — that’s good.

If you want, tell me:
- What kind of form this is (employer, tax form, bank, government benefits), and
- How the name on the document currently appears vs. your SSN card,

