Short answer: yes — in most situations your name should match the name the Social Security Administration (SSA) has on file (the name on your Social Security card). Exact or very close matches avoid verification problems, delays, and rejected forms.

Details and practical guidance
- Why exact matching matters
  - Agencies and employers verify your SSN against the SSA database. If the name on the form doesn’t match the SSA record, the SSN/name combination can be flagged as a mismatch.
  - For tax returns (IRS), employer paperwork (W‑2, I‑9), benefit applications, and many financial accounts, mismatches commonly cause processing delays or requests for additional documentation.
- What “match” means in practice
  - Exact legal name (first, middle, last) as recorded with SSA is safest.
  - Minor differences that usually don’t matter: capitalization, minor punctuation (hyphens, periods) and use of initials vs. full middle name are often tolerated — but not guaranteed.
  - Problematic differences: different last name (maiden vs. married), missing or extra middle names if SSA record differs, misspellings, transposed letters, omitted suffixes (Jr./Sr.) when they’re in the SSA record. These are more likely to cause a mismatch.
- If your name has changed or the SSA record is different
  - If you’ve legally changed your name but not updated SSA, update your record (SSA Form SS‑5 and supporting documents). Until you do, use the name on your Social Security card when completing forms that will be matched to SSA.
  - If the form allows an “also known as / previous name / maiden name” field, include that as well.
- What to do if you can’t use the exact SSA name on a form
  - Check the form instructions or ask the organization what they accept.
  - Attach or be ready to provide proof of identity/name change (Social Security card, driver’s license, marriage certificate, court order) if requested.
  - If you expect a mismatch, contact the recipient first to ask how they prefer it handled.
- Security note
  - Don’t share your full SSN in insecure channels. You already masked it here — that’s good practice.

If you want, tell me:
- What kind of form this is (tax, employer, bank, benefits, etc.) and whether your name differs from your Social Security card — I can give tailored wording to include on the form or a short message to the organization explaining the situation.

