Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: in most cases yes — use the exact legal name that’s on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Small variations sometimes pass, but mismatches can cause verification delays or rejections, so it’s safest to match exactly.

Details and practical guidance
- Why exact match matters: governments, employers, payroll systems, the IRS, and identity-verification services generally compare the name + SSN (or last 4) against SSA records. If the name you enter doesn’t match the SSA record, the check can fail and you may get a notice, a request for documentation, or a processing delay.
- What “exact” means in practice: include the same given name, middle name/initial (or lack of one), last name spelling, and any suffix (Jr., Sr., III) exactly as shown on your Social Security card. Hyphens, spaces, and punctuation sometimes matter for automated matches.
- Minor variations: some banks or background-check vendors will accept close variants (e.g., using a middle initial vs full middle name, or including/excluding a hyphen), but that’s not guaranteed. For tax and SSA/IRS matching you should be exact.
- If your current name isn’t on file with SSA: update your Social Security record before you submit forms that rely on name+SSN matching (e.g., new employer paperwork, tax forms). To update you’ll typically need original or certified documents (birth certificate, passport, marriage certificate, court order) and to visit or contact the SSA.
- If you can’t update immediately: when submitting the form, enter the name that matches the SSA record if possible and attach or upload supporting documentation or a note explaining the difference. For employment, notify HR/payroll so they can help resolve verification issues.
- When forms ask only for the last four digits of your SSN: those checks often use the last four + DOB + name; name still should match the SSA record used by the verifier. Also avoid sending full SSNs by email or insecure channels.

If you want, I can:
- Check the exact wording of the form you’re filling out and advise what to enter.
- Draft a short note/email you can send to HR, a bank, or the SSA explaining a name mismatch.
- Walk you through how to update your SSA record (what documents to gather).

