Short answer: usually—yes. For most official uses of your SSN (Social Security Administration records, tax reporting, employment verification, benefits, banks, background checks), the name you give should match the name on file with the SSA (your Social Security card) or the form will often be flagged for review or delayed.

Details and practical steps
- If the form specifically says “name as shown on Social Security card” or “legal name,” enter the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card.
- Minor differences (use/omission of a middle initial, punctuation like periods or hyphens) may sometimes be tolerated, but exact matches reduce the chance of delays or mismatches.
- Significant differences (maiden vs. married surname, different spelling, missing suffixes like Jr./Sr.) often trigger follow-up. If your name has changed, update SSA records first or include the former/other name if the form provides a field for that.
- For tax documents (W-2, 1099) the IRS primarily uses SSNs, but name mismatches between the IRS and SSA can cause processing delays or employer/IRS notices.
- For employment I-9 or bank ID checks, provide the name that matches the ID you present; if that differs from SSA records, you may need to show legal name-change documents.

What you can do next
1. Compare the form’s required name to the name on your Social Security card.
2. If they differ and you expect to keep the new name, update your SSA record (Form SS-5 and required documents: ID plus name-change proof such as marriage certificate or court order). See ssa.gov for details.
3. If you don’t want to change SSA records immediately, put the SSA name on the form (or use any “also known as / former name” field), or ask the organization what they require.
4. If you tell me which specific form it is (tax form, employer paperwork, bank account, application for benefits, etc.), I can give more tailored advice and wording suggestions.

Privacy note: you included the last four of your SSN. Avoid sharing your full SSN in messages. If you want, I can help draft a secure message to the agency or prepare the SSA name-change checklist. Which form are you filling out?

